1
00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:03.436
In this lesson, you'll learn about the four pillars of agent governance

2
00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.940
and how to apply them to your agentic workflow. Let's get started.

3
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.642
So what's the reality of building agents today?

4
00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:12.645
Well, they tend to start with prototype first development.

5
00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:16.649
So most systems start as POCs or as demos

6
00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:20.453
where feasibility takes priority over scalability and governance.

7
00:00:20.954 --> 00:00:23.656
And starting as a demo isn't necessarily a bad thing

8
00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:26.960
until performance starts taking priority over governance.

9
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.162
So how accurate the model is,

10
00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:32.265
the metrics that are happening with it are taking priority

11
00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:34.667
over access control, over auditing.

12
00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:37.871
So we don't want those to take a back seat to performance,

13
00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:40.673
especially when issues start emerging in production.

14
00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:42.509
We know the only way to

15
00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:44.677
really troubleshoot those issues is with governance.

16
00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:48.681
So having elevated permissions and access is a very bad thing

17
00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:50.884
when you're wanting to put something into production.

18
00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:53.553
Typically when developers start building,

19
00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:55.755
they get broad permissions across all environments

20
00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.058
instead of role-based access controls.

21
00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:01.261
Next is the reality of building agents today

22
00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:04.230
is there's not a lot of audit trails. So teams can't trace

23
00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.033
exactly what the agents did, what they had access to,

24
00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:10.236
where the agents failed, making debugging really difficult.

25
00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:14.040
So what happens is you have three times longer deployment cycles.

26
00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.975
You might have a really nice POC

27
00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:17.844
that took two or three weeks to build

28
00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:20.647
and then you give it to the team to put in production

29
00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:23.450
and it takes about eight months because they have to go back

30
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.853
and redo every aspect of security and governance.

31
00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.055
And you don't want that added security risk.

32
00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:32.058
So it's best to practice governance from day one.

33
00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:34.828
Now, what is data governance?

34
00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:38.932
Well, data governance is the framework of policies, processes, and standards

35
00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:42.535
that ensure data is accurate, it's safe, it's secure,

36
00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.637
and it's properly managed through its lifecycle.

37
00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:49.075
So Agent Governance is really the ability to have control plus visibility

38
00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:51.377
over your Lifecycle Management,

39
00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.580
so how you build and maintain your agent,

40
00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:57.283
Risk Management, so how you protect your agent against common failure modes,

41
00:01:57.283 --> 00:02:00.086
Security, so how you control access to your agent,

42
00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:02.889
and Observability, the ability to view all the actions

43
00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:04.891
that have been taken by your agent.

44
00:02:05.425 --> 00:02:07.827
So where does data governance take place

45
00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:10.230
in your ability to build agent systems?

46
00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:14.234
Well, basically whenever you're building an agent, you start with data prep.

47
00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:17.437
So this could be creating your tables,

48
00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:19.839
creating views and masks, creating a VectorDB.

49
00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:23.343
And then you're building the agent, so you're binding tools together,

50
00:02:23.343 --> 00:02:26.613
you're adding prompts, you're selecting your LLM.

51
00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:28.915
And then you're evaluating agents,

52
00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:31.918
either through LLM judges, through peer labeling,

53
00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:33.987
or having kind of that golden data

54
00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:36.055
set that you can evaluate your agents against,

55
00:02:36.356 --> 00:02:38.057
looking at tracing capabilities,

56
00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:40.460
having agent deployment is your next step.

57
00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.163
So making sure your agent's serving, you're tracking lineage.

58
00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:47.667
And from that beginning to end is the governance layer.

59
00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:50.170
So over all that, you need Lifecycle Management,

60
00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:52.972
Risk Management, Security, and Observability.

61
00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:55.675
So we're going to apply these layers of governance

62
00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:58.378
with the help of Unity Catalog and MLflow.

63
00:02:58.912 --> 00:03:01.114
As I mentioned before, governance encompasses

64
00:03:01.114 --> 00:03:04.617
everything you build from data creation to model monitoring.

65
00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:07.120
So I wanted to create a way

66
00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.622
for developers to conceptualize and categorize these areas.

67
00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:13.026
So that's when I've made these four pillars for you.

68
00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:16.229
These agent governance pillars are Lifecycle Management,

69
00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:18.932
Risk Management, Security, and Observability.

70
00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:21.201
And they have best practices.

71
00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:24.904
So for Lifecycle Management, the best practice is Separation of Duties.

72
00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:28.608
This enables multiple teams to manage data and model changes

73
00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:31.911
through dev, staging, and prod environments

74
00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:34.914
with version control to ensure proper review at each phase.

75
00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:38.151
Risk Management, which the best practice is Defense in Depth.

76
00:03:38.251 --> 00:03:41.454
So implementing multiple overlapping defense layers.

77
00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:46.554
This is your PII detection, guardrails, compliance controls, and monitoring

78
00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:48.695
in order to protect against issues

79
00:03:48.695 --> 00:03:50.930
anywhere from data ingestion to model performance.

80
00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:54.434
Security, the best practice here is Least Privilege Access.

81
00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:56.536
This ensures agents and users

82
00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:59.472
receive only the minimum permissions required for their role,

83
00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:04.177
implemented through encryption, authentication, and granular access controls.

84
00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:07.680
And then finally observability. The ability to audit everything.

85
00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.551
So this captures comprehensive logs of all system interactions,

86
00:04:11.551 --> 00:04:14.454
so data access, model actions, and predictions,

87
00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:17.757
enabling complete traceability and compliance reporting.

88
00:04:18.291 --> 00:04:20.193
Now I want to walk you through

89
00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:22.095
some of the common techniques and tools

90
00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:25.398
that implement that best practice of each pillar.

91
00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:29.502
So for Lifecycle Management and Separation of Duties,

92
00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:33.106
you're going to be using version control, CI/CD pipelines,

93
00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:37.110
Environment Management, so this is when you have your dev,

94
00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.079
staging, and prod environments in isolation.

95
00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.482
Deployment Orchestration,

96
00:04:42.482 --> 00:04:46.085
Change Management, so the ability to have rollback capabilities.

97
00:04:46.486 --> 00:04:48.955
Looking at Risk Management, your Defense in Depth.

98
00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:51.958
This is your data quality monitoring,

99
00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:55.161
your PII detection, these are your guardrails,

100
00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:58.765
any compliance capabilities and model validation.

101
00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:01.768
Security, your least privilege access,

102
00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:03.770
so this is your SSO login,

103
00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:05.738
these are your API keys,

104
00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:08.941
your multifactor authentication, service principals,

105
00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:11.744
secret management, access controls,

106
00:05:12.178 --> 00:05:15.782
data protection and your network security. And then your observability.

107
00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:18.351
So this is your ability to audit everything.

108
00:05:18.851 --> 00:05:21.454
In Gen AI, we often talk about OTel

109
00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:24.357
or the OpenTelemetry standard for logging traces from models.

110
00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:28.961
But this goes beyond that. This is logging audit logs, application logs,

111
00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:30.963
inference logging, access logs,

112
00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:32.765
monitoring capabilities,

113
00:05:32.965 --> 00:05:34.967
lineage tracking, and alerting.

114
00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:37.670
So those are the four main areas.

115
00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:41.674
And next time you want to build agent capabilities

116
00:05:41.674 --> 00:05:44.477
and you're wondering, is this ready for deployment?

117
00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:47.180
Is this ready for a production use case?

118
00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:50.383
I've created a checklist that you can go through

119
00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:53.186
just to keep these best practices in mind.

120
00:05:53.186 --> 00:05:55.855
For example, can you safely promote changes

121
00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:58.891
through environments with proper review and rollback capabilities?

122
00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:02.028
Do you have multiple layers of protection

123
00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:04.897
to catch issues before they impact production?

124
00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:08.067
Are all your data sources accessible only

125
00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:10.570
to authorized agents and authorized users?

126
00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:13.072
Can you trace every tool your agent used,

127
00:06:13.072 --> 00:06:16.442
when it ran and what data it accessed and what it returned?

128
00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:18.444
So these are the key capabilities

129
00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:21.047
for your Lifecycle Management, Risk Management,

130
00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:23.049
Security and Observability.

131
00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:26.452
You'll get to see these pillars in action throughout this short course.
